questions.prompt:
This Express-style handler runs on a long-lived Node server. Memory grows steadily until the process crashes after a few hours. Find the leak(s) and fix them.

```js
const seen = {};
const bus = new EventEmitter();

app.get("/report/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const big = await loadReport(req.params.id); // ~1 MB
  seen[req.params.id] = big;
  bus.on("refresh", () => console.log("refresh", big.title));
  res.json(summarize(big));
});
```

questions.answer:
Two leaks. (1) `seen[id] = big` stores every report in a module-level object that is never cleared, so memory grows with the number of distinct ids. Replace it with a bounded LRU cache (e.g. max 100 entries) or drop it. (2) `bus.on("refresh", ...)` adds one listener per request; each listener's closure captures `big` (~1 MB), and the emitter holds all of them forever. Register a single listener at startup, or if it must be per-request, remove it when the work finishes:

```js
const onRefresh = () => console.log("refresh", big.title);
bus.on("refresh", onRefresh);
res.on("finish", () => bus.off("refresh", onRefresh));
```

**Use a `WeakMap`, with the user object as the key.**

```js
const meta = new WeakMap();
function getMeta(user) {
  if (!meta.has(user)) meta.set(user, computeExpensive(user));
  return meta.get(user);
}
```

**Why not `Map`:** a `Map` holds **strong** references to its keys. As long as the map exists (a module-level cache lives forever), every user object put in it stays reachable from a root, so the GC can never free it or its metadata. With thousands of users per hour, memory grows without limit: a leak.

**Why `WeakMap` works:** it holds keys **weakly**. Its reference doesn't count for reachability. When the rest of the program drops the last reference to a user, the GC collects the user **and** its entry automatically.

**Limits:** keys must be **objects** (not strings or numbers), and a WeakMap is **not iterable** and has no `.size`. Entries can vanish at any GC, so listing them would give results that depend on GC timing.

**When to use a bounded LRU instead:**

- Keys are **strings/ids** (e.g. `userId`), which WeakMap can't hold.
- You need **predictable memory** (e.g. "max 10,000 entries, ~50 MB") and explicit **eviction** (least recently used first, and/or a TTL so data doesn't go stale).
- You want the cache to survive even when no one holds the object.

**Trade-off:** WeakMap ties entry lifetime to **GC timing** and object lifetime, with zero bookkeeping. An LRU gives you **explicit control** over size and freshness, but you choose and tune the limits.
